    "notes": "Client-side tagging means the tracking code runs in the visitor's browser (the client). The browser loads the page, the Google tag or GTM container runs, and the browser sends a small request (\"hit\") to Google's collection servers, for example google-analytics.com/g/collect. Everything that happens in the browser — page views, clicks, scrolls, form submissions — can be observed by the tag because it runs on the same page as the visitor. The limitation is that the browser is not under your control: ad blockers, privacy settings, slow connections and cookie restrictions can all stop or change what is sent. This is why newer approaches such as the Google tag gateway and server-side tagging exist (see the \"What's New\" section).",
