    "notes": "First-party vs third-party: when you are on shop.com, shop.com is the first party. Any other website the page contacts — for example googletagmanager.com, which delivers Google's tracking code — is a third party. The problem: many ad blockers and browser privacy features block requests to third-party tracking sites. When that happens, the GTM or Google tag code never loads, and that visit is missing from GA4 and Google Ads. The solution — the Google tag gateway for advertisers: the website serves Google's code from a path on its own domain, for example shop.com/metrics. The browser now asks shop.com for the code (first party). The website's hosting network (CDN, such as Cloudflare, Akamai or Fastly) or load balancer quietly forwards those requests to Google and returns the answer. Look at the snippet line on the slide: the only visible difference is the address — www.googletagmanager.com becomes shop.com/metrics. The container ID (GTM-ABC1234) stays the same. History: launched as \"first-party mode\" with Cloudflare in late 2024; renamed Google tag gateway and opened to everyone in May 2025; more hosting providers (Akamai, Fastly, Google Cloud, Amazon CloudFront) added in 2026. Ethics and law: the gateway changes where the code is loaded from. It does not give you permission to collect more data — cookie consent and privacy laws still apply. Do not confuse it with server-side tagging (next slide): the gateway only passes Google's requests through your domain; server-side tagging runs your own tagging server that can change data before it is sent.",
