    "text": [
     "Which one should a business use? Small websites: client-side is enough. Large online shops with big ad budgets or strict privacy rules usually use BOTH — the web container collects the data, the server container sends it on.",
     " | Client-side | Server-side",
     "Where do the tags run? | In the visitor's browser | On the company's own server",
     "Requests from the browser | One per company (many) | One, to the company's own domain",
     "Page speed | Slower with many tags | Faster — fewer scripts",
     "Ad blockers | Often block requests | Block far fewer requests",
     "Control over data | Each company collects directly | Company can clean data first",
     "Cost and skills | Free; marketers can set it up | Monthly server cost; technical setup"
    ],
    "notes": "Compare the two architectures: Client-side: the browser runs all vendor tags and sends data directly to each vendor. Easy and free, but each script slows the page, and you cannot control what each vendor collects once its script runs. Server-side: you run a server container (e.g., on Google Cloud) on a subdomain like metrics.yoursite.com. The browser sends one data stream there. \"Clients\" in the server container receive the data; server tags forward it to GA4, Google Ads and others. You can remove IP addresses or hash personal data before it leaves your server. Trade-offs: server-side costs money (hosting) and needs technical skills, but improves performance, control and data durability. Many companies use both: a web container to collect, a server container to process and distribute.",
