    "text": [
     "A Document Object Model (DOM) tree is a hierarchical representation of an HTML document. It consists of a root node, which is the document itself, and a series of child nodes that represent the elements, attributes, and text content of the document. Each node in the tree has a parent node, except for the root node, and can have multiple child nodes. The DOM in Google Analytics refers to the structure and hierarchy of the web page elements on which tracking scripts are executed. Here's how it relates to Google Analytics:",
     "Data Collection: When you implement Google Analytics tracking code on your website, JavaScript code is executed in the user's web browser. This code interacts with the DOM to gather data about user interactions, page views, events, and other relevant information."
    ],
    "notes": "The Document Object Model (DOM) is the browser's live tree representation of a web page. The root is the document; below it are elements such as <html>, <head>, <body>, headings, links and text nodes. Each node has one parent (except the root) and can have children. Why analysts care: GTM tags and triggers work by reading this tree. A Click trigger checks properties of the clicked node (its text, classes, ID, URL); a DOM Element variable reads the text of a specific node; an Element Visibility trigger watches whether a node appears on screen. Try it: in Chrome, right-click any element → Inspect. The Elements panel shows the DOM tree.",
