    "title": "Web Scraping in Web Analytics",
    "text": [
     "Web scraping refers to the automated process of collecting and parsing data from web pages. Instead of relying on human browsing, a scraper (program or script) mimics the behavior of a browser: it requests the page, reads the HTML source code, identifies relevant elements, and extracts desired content such as product titles, prices, reviews, or metadata.",
     "In formal terms, scraping is an application of data retrieval and transformation from unstructured or semi-structured sources into structured datasets."
    ],
    "notes": "Web scraping automates what a person does when copying information from web pages: a program requests a page, parses its HTML, finds the elements it needs (for example, product names and prices) and stores them as structured data such as a table. Marketing uses include competitor price monitoring, review collection for sentiment analysis, and building product datasets. Always respect a website's terms of service, robots.txt and privacy laws, and avoid overloading servers with requests.",
    "images": []
