    "n": 60,
    "title": "Web Scraping in Web Analytics",
    "text": [
     "The contemporary digital ecosystem generates vast volumes of user and content data every second. Organizations seek to capture, interpret, and act upon this data to gain competitive advantage, optimize user experience, and drive marketing performance. Two technological disciplines underpin this effort: web scraping and web analytics.",
     "At first glance, these appear distinct — scraping extracts data from websites, while analytics collects behavioral data about users on websites. Yet conceptually, both rely on the same structural foundation: HTML, CSS, and the Document Object Model (DOM).",
     "In both domains, the analyst must identify, locate, and interpret elements in the DOM — whether to extract content (scraping) or to monitor interactions (analytics). Understanding the technical mechanisms of scraping thus strengthens an analyst’s ability to measure user behavior accurately via Google Tag Manager (GTM) and related analytics platforms."
    ],
    "notes": "Web scraping and web analytics look different — one extracts content, the other measures behaviour — but they share the same foundation: HTML, CSS and the DOM. A scraper asks \"where is the price on this page so I can copy it?\" A GTM trigger asks \"where is the Buy button so I can detect clicks on it?\" Both answer the question with a CSS selector or an element path. This is why analysts who understand how pages are built can track almost any interaction without waiting for developers.",
